.TH POWER 8
.SH NAME
power \- what the processor package draws, how deep it idles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aux/power
[
.B -i
.I seconds
]
[
.B -n
.I count
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Aux/power
reads the processor's model-specific registers
.RB ( #P/msr ,
see
.IR arch (3))
every
.I seconds
(default 5) and prints a line:
the average power of the RAPL domains the processor has
.RB ( pkg ,
the whole package;
.BR cores ;
.BR gpu ,
the integrated graphics;
.BR dram ;
on AMD processors the package and the cores summed),
then the share of the time the package spent in its C-states
.RB ( pc2
to
.BR pc10 )
and the cores, averaged over the processors, in theirs
.RB ( cc3 ,
.BR cc6 ,
.BR cc7 ;
Intel only).
A register the processor lacks is left out.
With
.B -n
it stops after
.I count
lines.
.PP
It reads each processor's registers on that processor, wiring itself
to one after the other
.RI ( proc (3)),
and needs to open
.BR #P/msr ,
as the host owner.
.SH EXAMPLE
A ThinkPad X1 Carbon (Broadwell) idling in C1 only:
.IP
.EX
% aux/power -i 5 -n 1
pkg 2.29W  cores 0.11W  gpu 0.01W  dram 0.84W  pc2 0%  pc3 0% ...
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/power.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pstate (8),
.IR arch (3)
.SH BUGS
The battery's own discharge rate, the whole machine's draw, is not
shown:
.IR acpi (8)'s
.B battery
file has no rate.
